  "body": "## What this changes\n\nv5.27.0 added a `window` preference to `desktop.json` and a `--window` flag, but **the dashboard could not set it**. The endpoint carried the value through every save without ever accepting one, so the CLI was the only way to change it.\n\nThe Deployment section already existed, so this adds a control to it rather than building a second home for desktop settings.\n\nBeside the select, a read-only line reports what `auto` actually resolves to on this machine — `auto → app-mode (Microsoft Edge)` — because a preference of \"auto\" otherwise tells you nothing about what you will get.\n\nEach option states its real consequence, and the embedded-webview option says plainly that **OAuth login, downloads and copy buttons may not work in it**. That is the whole reason app-mode is the default, so burying it is the one thing that hint must not do.\n\n## The import boundary, resolved by moving\n\nResolution had to be readable from the API, and `api` may not import `cli` (`tests/contracts/test_import_boundaries.py`). Rather than crossing the edge or keeping a second copy, the Chromium lookup and availability logic **moved** from `cli/desktop_window.py` to `config/desktop.py`, which both may import — a complete migration, not a shim. Import-boundary contract: **19 passed**.\n\n## Verification\n\n**Full suite: 6 failed, 4294 passed, 26 skipped** — the six documented PowerShell installer failures, identical to unmodified `fork/main` measured in the same shell. Zero new failures. ruff format, ruff check, ty: clean.\n\n**Live server on a throwaway home, real HTTP:**\n\n```\nset window=browser   -> persists to desktop.json\nunrelated save  x2   -> \"window\":\"browser\"   (preservation holds)\ninvalid value        -> HTTP 400 {\"detail\":\"Invalid window preference\"}, does not stick\nresolved             -> window_auto_provider=\"app-mode\", window_auto_reason=\"Microsoft Edge\"\nserved HTML          -> the select is present with all four options\n```\n\n## One defect fixed on review\n\n`_desktop_state_response` called `resolve_auto_window()` inline on an `async def` handler. Measured **43 ms median, 75 ms max**, and it does not get cheaper on repeat because `shutil.which` is the cost rather than the import — so every desktop request stalled the event loop for that long. All three call sites now hand it to a thread, with the measurement recorded beside it so nobody simplifies it back.\n\n## A false bug worth recording\n\nA live reproduction \"showed\" the preference resetting on every unrelated save, and the suite showed a matching **seventh** failure — three signals agreeing. All three were artifacts of starting a server from the worktree venv while `uv run pytest` was reinstalling the package into that same venv. Run with nothing else touching the venv, the behaviour is correct and the suite sits at baseline. Two concurrent `uv run` invocations against one worktree fight over its `.venv`; anything measured during the overlap is measuring a half-installed package.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **No DOM was executed.** The dashboard was exercised over real HTTP against a running server and the select's presence is asserted in the served markup — but `admin.js` was not run, so the wiring is verified by reading plus the API round trip, not by clicking.\n- `resolve_auto_window()` is uncached. It is called once per page load and once per save, now off the event loop, so caching would add invalidation risk for no measurable gain.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
